{"role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_Lup24jEKTYDbGiz4ekiDuY2l","function":{"name":"Write","arguments":"{\"path\":\"ANSWER.md\",\"content\":\"# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's own zones\\n\\n## Symptom\\n\\nEvery HR corridor from event 184233 rendered on the watch as one of the\\nForerunner 965's own auto-detected zone bands (118–137, 151–162, 137–151),\\nnever the planned range (117–129, 151–160, 129–141 — i.e. % LTHR × 172).\\nChanging the watch's LTHR moved the bands but they stayed zone edges.\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\n`gen.py` emitted HR targets as `{\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"value\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}`.\\nWith that key shape, Intervals' FIT export routes the target as a\\n**Lap-HR / zone-mapped** target: the watch clamps it to its own stored HR\\nzones instead of displaying the bpm range Intervals computed. The API accepts\\nthe body without complaint (the readback shows it stored verbatim), so the\\nfailure is silent until it lands on the watch.\\n\\n## What I changed\\n\\nOne line in `gen.py:13` (`hr_target`):\\n\\n```diff\\n-    return {\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"value\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}\\n+    return {\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"start\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}\\n```\\n\\n`{start, end}` switches the FIT export to an **Instant-HR corridor**, which\\nthe watch displays as the raw bpm Intervals computed from its own stored LTHR\\n(172 for this athlete) — drift-immune and independent of the watch's zones.\\nPercentages (`%lthr`) are kept rather than absolute bpm, so Intervals\\nconverts at export time from the threshold it has on file.\\n\\nVerified: `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits `start`/`end` on\\nevery step, including inside the repeat block. End-to-end watch verification\\nrequires a push + Garmin sync, which is out of scope per the ground rules —\\nthe readback in `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still shows the old\\n`value` shape, so a re-push is needed for the fix to reach the watch.\\n\\n## Parts of the colleague's note that applied\\n\\n- **The core diagnosis and fix**: `{value, end}` vs `{start, end}` selects\\n  Lap-HR/zone-mapped vs Instant-HR routing in the FIT export. This matched\\n  our symptom exactly (corridors pinned to the watch's zone bands) and the\\n  one-word rename `value` → `start` is the fix. This is precisely the\\n  breakthrough they described.\\n- **The earlier-failure context**: their tries 1 and 2 (`units: \\\"bpm\\\"` →\\n  impossible 214–249 bpm; `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` → constant 101 fallback)\\n  match the failed approaches recorded in `readback/watch-notes.md`, which\\n  corroborates that we were hitting the same routing behavior.\\n- **Percentages over absolute bpm**: they recommend `%lthr` units so\\n  Intervals converts at export from its own stored threshold — we already\\n  do this and kept it.\\n\\n## Parts that did not apply\\n\\n- **The top-level `description` text-grammar trap** (steps invisible in the\\n  Intervals web UI, `m` = minutes, `mtr`/`km` for distance, bare `Z2` =\\n  power zones, leading digits eaten as durations). Our reported symptom is\\n  only the watch-side zone clamping, and the readback shows\\n  `workout_doc.steps` populated and returned as sent — the parser accepted\\n  the event. The web-UI visibility problem they describe isn't one we\\n  observed, so I made no description changes; adding one would be untested\\n  guesswork at Intervals' grammar.\\n- **Their \\\"absolute bpm is impossible\\\" detour**: irrelevant — we never\\n  needed absolute bpm once `%lthr` + `start` was known to work.\\n\"}"}}]}
