{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. It was the exact trap your colleague described.\n\n**Root cause:** `gen.py:13` built HR targets as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. That key shape makes Intervals' FIT export route the target as a zone-mapped Lap-HR target, so the watch clamps it to its own auto-detected zones — exactly the symptom in `readback/watch-notes.md` (every corridor landed on a watch band: 118–137, 151–162, 137–151, never the planned 151–160 or 129–141).\n\n**The fix** — one word in `gen.py:13`:\n\n```diff\n-    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n+    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n```\n\n`{start, end}` routes as an Instant-HR corridor, which the watch displays as the raw bpm Intervals computed from its own LTHR (172) — independent of the watch's zones. Kept `%lthr` percentages per the colleague's advice so Intervals converts at export. Verified locally: `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits `start`/`end` on every step, including inside the repeat block. Nothing was pushed to Intervals (ground rules), and `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still shows the old `value` shape — a re-push through `push.sh` is needed for the fix to actually reach the watch.\n\nFull write-up of what changed, why, and which parts of the colleague's note applied (core `value`→`start` diagnosis, the matching earlier-failure history, `%lthr` percentages) vs. didn't (the top-level `description` text-grammar trap — our symptom was only watch-side clamping and the API accepted the event fine) is in `ANSWER.md`."}
